@node mixing-channels "Mixing channels"
@{b}zz9000ax.audio@{ub} 4.29 advertises up to @{b}8 mixing channels@{ub} per mode
(@{b}AHIDB_MaxChannels@{ub} was 1 before, which is why the channel selector in AHI
Prefs was greyed out). The driver is an @{b}AHISF_MIXING@{ub} subdriver: AHI mixes
all allocated channels in software on the Amiga's CPU - linear
interpolation, 32-bit accumulation - and the card still receives a single
stereo PCM stream, exactly as before. Nothing in the hardware transport,
the fabric lease, the legacy register path, or MHI coexistence changes with
the channel count; the ceiling is a host CPU budget, not a hardware limit.

Practical guidance:

- The actual count is chosen per mode in AHI Prefs; CPU cost accrues only
  for channels that are actively playing (silent channels take AHI's cheap
  silence path).

- 68030: 2-4 channels at 44.1/48 kHz. 68040: 4-8. 68060 / Apollo core:

  8. (The community two-channel variant of the stock 4.19 driver -

Xanxi/Fitzsteve, same one-line change - was validated with ZZQuake on a
68030/25.)

- Overcommitting shows up as AHI CPU-limiter period skips - audible stutter
  - not as a driver failure. If 8 proves comfortable on your hardware,
  raising the ceiling further is a one-line change (@{b}ZZ_AX_AHI_MAX_CHANNELS@{ub}
  in @{b}driver/zz9000ax-ahi.c@{ub}).

UAE's AHI is not a yardstick here: its m68k-side driver advertises 8
channels while the actual mixing runs on the host CPU, so emulated channel
counts are effectively free.

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node ahi-timer-pairing "AHI timer pairing"
Version 4.28 calls @{b}PostTimer@{ub} after every executed @{b}PreTimer@{ub}, including when
AHI's CPU limiter skips mixing. Both fabric and legacy playback use this
pairing. Earlier versions left the previous exit timestamp stale on a skip,
which could incorrectly suppress subsequent periods. The CPU limit, 50-Hz
pacing, startup runway and buffer bounds are unchanged.

The target regression in @{b}driver/timer_pair_test.c@{ub} includes the real driver.
It checks recovery of complete, ordered PCM after an overload skip in both
paths, and balanced timing without delivery for oversized legacy periods.
Scheduling, hook dispatch and coherent RAM are controlled by the fixture;
this does not qualify real interrupt, DMA or cache timing.

After the normal AHI build has staged the SDK headers, run from the repo
root (the executable can also run on AmigaOS):

tools/amiga-docker.sh ahi/driver m68k-amigaos-gcc \
  timer_pair_test.c asmfuncs.s -m68020 -O3 \
  -I../../include -Izz9k-headers -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections \
  -Wl,--gc-sections -Wall -Wextra -Werror -Wno-unused-parameter \
  -o timer_pair_test -ldebug -lamiga -noixemul
vamos --cpu=68020 --ram-size=8192 --stack-size=64 ahi/driver/timer_pair_test

The emulator verification used @{b}amitools==0.8.1@{ub} and @{b}machine68k==0.3.0@{ub}. All
three cases must pass. Hardware still needs a separate playback and
recording check; a corrected timing pair does not diagnose a weak analog
return.

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node ahi-recording "AHI recording"
@{b}zz9000ax.audio@{ub} supports stereo recording from the ZZ9000AX RCA inputs when
used with firmware that exposes the AX receive-status register. Firmware
without that capability continues to provide playback, but the driver does
not advertise AHI recording.

Set both ZZ9000AX auxiliary jumpers to @{b}IN@{ub} before recording. AHI sees one
fixed-gain input named @{b}RCA In@{ub}; samples are delivered as signed 16-bit
stereo at the selected AudioMode rate. Playback and recording may be
started and stopped independently, including full-duplex operation.

The production capture path uses a TDM8 slot-0/1 bridge ahead of the
existing I2S receive formatter, DMA ring, and receive interrupt. It
therefore requires a matching FPGA bitstream, ARM firmware, and
@{b}zz9000ax.audio@{ub} build. The register and buffer contract is documented in
@{b}docs/ahi-recording-spec.md@{ub} (../docs/ahi-recording-spec.md).

For the production full-duplex hardware gate, build and run @{b}ZZAXDuplexTest@{ub}
(duplextest/README.md). It starts playback and recording together on one
low-level AHI control, unlike two-client tests involving AHI Record and a
separate player.

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node hardware-revisions "Hardware revisions"
Early ZZ9000AX @{b}Revision 1@{ub} boards carry an opamp at @{b}U4@{ub} on the Paula
pass-through path that over-amplifies Paula's line-in before the mixer. The
practical effect is that raw Paula drowns out AHI and MHI playback even
with the mixer set symmetric - MP3 / MOD sound noticeably quieter than the
same Amiga's chip-audio through the ZZ9000AX. MNT resolved this in
subsequent revisions by @{b}removing U4@{ub}.

Newer revisions omit U4, but that alone does not qualify every card's
default gain as distortion-free. With no saved audio settings, current
firmware uses the one-card-informed Paula/AX ceiling fallback 48/80 and
parity baseline 36/72 (Level 132/160). Saved settings take precedence,
including older louder pairs; check ZZTop before testing.

If you have an unfixed R1:

1. @{b}Desolder U4@{ub} - MNT's analog-path fix, if you're comfortable with SMD
   rework. A digital mixer or limiter cannot undo analog distortion already
   produced before its input.

2. @{b}Measure and set each clean ceiling and baseline@{ub} - matched firmware
   exposes these controls in ZZTop's Audio window. The qualified R1 card
   measured Paula 48 / AX 80. With the permanent post-mix limiter the
   boundary for that calibrated pair is 160; firmware caps each leg at its
   own ceiling first. The older three-quarter-of-AX boundary (60 for 48/80)
   is pre-limiter history. Save the card's own measurements, not the
   example pair.

The former @{b}ENV:ZZ9K_MIX_LEVELS@{ub} register override was removed; see
@{"Runtime tunables" link runtime-tunables-env-variables} below.

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node firmware-authoritative-control-plane "Firmware-authoritative control plane"
Both drivers are clients of the firmware's audio control plane. When
zz9k.library is present @{b}and@{ub} the running firmware advertises the
audio-control capability, allocating the device submits the owner's neutral
source trim - the reserved keep-baseline word, "no trim from this owner" -
through the control-plane mailbox, and the same word is resubmitted at
release. The firmware owns every master-chain write (LPF, mixer volume,
EQ): neither driver stamps DSP state at allocate, Play start, or release,
so a dialed-in scene survives apps opening and closing the device.

The MHI app mixer API (@{b}MHISetParam@{ub} for volume, panning, prefactor, and the
EQ bands) is legacy-only against pre-control-plane firmware: those
parameters map straight onto the master chain the scene module owns, so on
control-plane firmware the call reports the documented not-supported status
and the chain is left alone - use scenes (ZZTop's Audio window) instead.
The calibrated matched firmware advertises the capability after its
hardware gate. Clients still require a matched pair and retain these
mixed-version fallbacks:

- @{b}New driver + old firmware@{ub} - no control surface, no trims: the driver
  detects the absent capability and falls back to legacy playback with the
  old anti-alias stamps (the AHI LPF at half the mix rate, the MHI 20000 Hz
  LPF at Play start), so old firmware behaves as before. Audio works; the
  Paula/AX balance is whatever the firmware's own state leaves it at (on an
  unfixed early R1 that can mean loud Paula - see @{"Hardware revisions" link hardware-revisions}). The
  balance remedy still needs the matched firmware. If @{b}ENV:ZZ9K_MIX_LEVELS@{ub}
  is still set, each driver prints one load-time line on the debug channel
  (Sashimi/serial) saying the variable is ignored and that the balance
  remedy needs the matched firmware.

- @{b}Old driver + new firmware@{ub} - the old driver's DSP register stamps (LPF at
  allocate/Play, mixer volume) are rejected by the firmware's
  scene-authority gate and playback continues, but the legacy anti-alias
  LPF tracking no longer happens: the cutoff stays where the active scene
  put it until the drivers are updated. Early-R1 baseline/calibration is
  unavailable until the matched drivers expose the control surface.

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node runtime-tunables-env-variables "Runtime tunables (ENV variables)"
The variable below is optional. Unset it to get the default behavior. Its
value is read on each @{b}AllocAudio@{ub} (AHI) or @{b}AllocDecoder@{ub} (MHI) call, so
changing it takes effect the next time an app opens the device - no reboot
required.

@{b}ENV:ZZ9K_MIX_LEVELS@{ub} @{i}(AHI + MHI)@{ui} and @{b}ENV:ZZ9000AX-NOLPF@{ub} @{i}(AHI only)@{ui} were
@{b}removed@{ub}. Balance intent now flows through the firmware control plane's
operator baseline and LPF intent through the active scene (see
@{"Firmware-authoritative control plane" link firmware-authoritative-control-plane}). Neither driver reads either
variable; if @{b}ZZ9K_MIX_LEVELS@{ub} is still set, the drivers print a one-line
load-time notice on the debug channel (Sashimi/serial) that it is ignored.
Delete the stale variables (@{b}Unsetenv ZZ9K_MIX_LEVELS@{ub}, @{b}Unsetenv
ZZ9000AX-NOLPF@{ub}, plus their @{b}ENVARC:@{ub} copies if you used @{b}setenv SAVE@{ub}) when
updating.

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node env-zz9k-int2-ahi-mhi "ENV:ZZ9K_INT2 (AHI + MHI)"
If this variable exists, both drivers attach their interrupt server to @{b}INT2@{ub}
(@{b}INTB_PORTS@{ub}) instead of the default @{b}INT6@{ub} (@{b}INTB_EXTER@{ub}). Useful if something
else on your system is monopolising INT6 (some 68060 accelerator boards,
certain SCSI controllers, poorly-behaved networking hardware). The two
drivers @{b}must@{ub} agree - AHI and MHI coordinate on the same interrupt line, so
this setting has to be consistent for both or they won't see each other's
ISR and the mutual-exclusion check will break. Setting the variable
configures both drivers uniformly; don't partially enable it.

setenv ZZ9K_INT2 1

With firmware 2.3+ the preferred home for this option is @{b}int2 = on@{ub} in the
SD card's @{b}ZZ9000.CFG@{ub} (editable from ZZTop's Settings window), which
configures AHI, MHI and ZZ9000Net uniformly. An existing @{b}ENV:ZZ9K_INT2@{ub}
variable still takes precedence over the config file, so remove the ENV
variable when migrating.

@{"Back" link runtime-tunables-env-variables}

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node troubleshooting "Troubleshooting"
@{b}Symptom              | Likely cause / fix                                  @{ub}
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Paula much louder    | Early R1 (U4 opamp). Desolder U4, or - on matched   
than MP3/MOD through | firmware - set the operator baseline in ZZTop's     
the card             | Audio window.                                       

Muffled / dull AHI   | On control-plane firmware the LPF is scene-owned:   
output at low sample | raise the scene LPF cutoff in ZZTop's Audio window  
rates                | (the old @{b}ENV:ZZ9000AX-NOLPF@{ub} bypass was removed; on  
                     | pre-control-plane firmware the legacy half-rate LPF 
                     | stamp applies as before).                           

ZZTop's Audio button | The firmware does not advertise the audio-control   
is greyed out        | capability (pre-verification builds), or the AX     
                     | daughterboard is absent. Update to the matched      
                     | firmware release that advertises it; playback itself
                     | is unaffected.                                      

ZZTop says "Audio:   | The firmware did not return a valid audio boundary. 
control state        | Reopen Audio once the control service responds; no  
unavailable - retry" | audio settings were changed.                        

ZZTop says           | Audio edits and Save pause while the firmware       
"Calibration         | confirms the new ceiling pair; the one-second       
awaiting firmware    | Audio-window timer retries the read. If it remains  
state..."            | pending, close and reopen Audio to read the live    
                     | state.                                              

"Can't allocate!     | The other driver owns the card. Close whatever      
Hardware already     | MHI/AHI app is running first.                       
used by MHI/AHI."    |                                                     

Audio device fails   | INT6 conflict. @{b}setenv ZZ9K_INT2 1@{ub} to move both      
to open on specific  | drivers to INT2.                                    
accelerators         |                                                     

Short random burst   | Fixed in recent commits (driver now silences the DAC
before playback on   | at allocate time). Update to the latest             
first app open       | @{b}zz9000ax.audio@{ub}.                                     

AHI application has  | Update both the ARM firmware and @{b}zz9000ax.audio@{ub}; old
no recording option  | firmware is intentionally detected as playback-only.

Recording is silent  | Move both ZZ9000AX auxiliary jumpers to @{b}IN@{ub} and      
                     | select @{b}RCA In@{ub}.                                      

